For simple cases, **ad-hoc `attributePaths`** directly on the repository method is the cleanest approach. No entity annotation needed.

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"customer", "items"})
    List<Order> findByStatus(String status);
}
```

This tells Hibernate to LEFT JOIN `customer` and `items` when loading orders by status. The same repository can have another method without `@EntityGraph` that loads orders lazily — **each method controls its own fetch strategy.**

For graphs reused across multiple repositories or services, **`@NamedEntityGraph`** on the entity class is cleaner. Define the graph once on the entity, reference it by name in repository methods. This avoids duplicating `attributePaths` strings across multiple methods.

**For paginated queries with collections**, avoid `@EntityGraph` entirely. Use `@BatchSize` on the association instead, or split the query into two: first load parent IDs with pagination, then load the full entities with `@EntityGraph` using the ID list.

The repository must extend `JpaSpecificationExecutor<T>` alongside `JpaRepository<T, ID>`. This adds `findAll(Specification<T>, Pageable)` and related methods.

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long>,
        JpaSpecificationExecutor<Order> {}
```

Each filter becomes a static method returning `Specification<Order>`. The lambda receives `Root`, `CriteriaQuery`, and `CriteriaBuilder` — standard JPA Criteria API types.

```java
public class OrderSpecs {
    public static Specification<Order> hasStatus(String status) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> cb.equal(root.get("status"), status);
    }
    public static Specification<Order> createdAfter(LocalDate date) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), date);
    }
}
```

At the service layer, compose only the filters the user provided. `Specification.where(null)` is a no-op that starts the chain cleanly, and each `.and()` adds a filter only if the parameter is present. This eliminates the combinatorial explosion of derived query methods while keeping every query parameterized and type-safe.

**Class-based projections** (records or DTOs) are the most explicit. Declare a record with the fields you need, and Spring Data maps the query result to the constructor.

```java
public record UserSummary(Long id, String name, String email) {}

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    List<UserSummary> findByActiveTrue();
}
```

Spring generates `SELECT u.id, u.name, u.email FROM User u WHERE u.active = true` — only three columns, no entity management. This is the most efficient approach.

**Closed interface projections** achieve the same efficiency with an interface. Spring generates a proxy. The advantage is that one entity can have multiple projection interfaces for different views. **Dynamic projections** with `<T> List<T> findByActiveTrue(Class<T> type)` let callers choose which projection to use at call time.

**Open projections** with `@Value("#{target.firstName + ' ' + target.lastName}")` compute values from the entity — but Spring loads all columns and the full entity to evaluate the SpEL expression. Use open projections only when you genuinely need computed properties.

`@Modifying` requires `@Transactional` and `@Query` with an UPDATE or DELETE statement.

```java
@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true, flushAutomatically = true)
@Query("UPDATE Order o SET o.status = :status WHERE o.createdAt < :cutoff")
int archiveOldOrders(@Param("status") String status, @Param("cutoff") LocalDate cutoff);
```

**`flushAutomatically = true`** flushes pending changes before the bulk operation. Without it, pending entity changes are lost — the bulk UPDATE runs against the database state, not the persistence context state. If you modified an order's price in the same transaction, that change is lost when the flush happens after the bulk operation.

**`clearAutomatically = true`** evicts all managed entities after the operation. Without it, reads in the same transaction return stale cached values.

For audited entities, **don't use `@Modifying`** if audit callbacks must fire. Load entities, modify them, and let Hibernate's dirty checking generate individual UPDATEs that trigger `@PreUpdate`. The per-entity approach is slower (N updates vs 1 bulk update) but ensures audit logging works correctly.

The base entity contains all four audit fields. Use `Instant` for timestamps instead of `LocalDateTime` — `Instant` is timezone-independent and unambiguous across server locations.

```java
@MappedSuperclass
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
public abstract class AuditableEntity {
    @CreatedDate
    private Instant createdAt;
    @LastModifiedDate
    private Instant updatedAt;
    @CreatedBy
    private String createdBy;
    @LastModifiedBy
    private String updatedBy;
}
```

The `AuditorAware` bean supplies the current user. For Spring Security, it reads from `SecurityContextHolder`. For system operations (schedulers, message consumers), set a system principal before the transactional method.

Enable auditing in a configuration class with `@EnableJpaAuditing`. This registers the `AuditingEntityListener` globally. **Without `@EnableJpaAuditing`, the annotations are ignored silently** — timestamps remain null, and the bug is invisible until you check the database.
